event: final
data: {"type":"final","sequence":0,"request_id":"b442d426-d5c4-4fff-8d01-64dd3c9658d3","status":true,"message":"Chat turn processed successfully.","timestamp":"2026-08-14T09:14:21.114589+00:00","data":{"ai_message":"Yes — I’ve converted the approved LearnSphere framework into a complete research brief. The study is designed to identify why learners disengage or fail to complete courses, while separating the needs of current learners, inactive learners, potential users, parents, teachers, and administrators. It combines learner-experience measurement, usage and attitude analysis, churn diagnosis, and feature-prioritization logic without introducing unsupported stimulus testing. Please review the brief below and let me know if you would like to adjust the audience, sample size, or methodology before the survey is generated.","chat_id":"e024bd98-0387-4ac2-87a9-434265ef3921","guest_id":null,"chat_turn_id":"759079c0-3240-4d53-9768-3511e8ea3d41","chat_name":"New Chat (2026-08-14 09:08)","turn_number":8,"survey_turn_number":null,"questions":[],"media_request":null,"research_brief":"# Research Brief: LearnSphere Learner Engagement and Completion Study\n\n### **Background & Context**\nLearnSphere needs to understand why learners do not sustain engagement or complete courses, and which parts of the learning experience most influence continued progress. The research is triggered by learner complaints, low engagement or completion, upcoming feature planning, competitive pressure, and possible expansion into certifications or institutional partnerships.\n\nIndia’s digital-learning environment includes both commercial platforms and large public digital-learning systems. PM e-VIDYA is designed to provide multi-mode digital and online education access to nearly 25 crore school-going children, according to the Government of India. ([pmevidya.education.gov.in](https://pmevidya.education.gov.in/)) DIKSHA also demonstrates the scale and institutional relevance of curriculum-linked digital learning, with extensive state, union-territory, teacher, and learner participation, according to the Government of India’s DIKSHA platform and parliamentary reporting. ([diksha.gov.in](https://diksha.gov.in/), [sansad.in](https://sansad.in/getFile/annex/269/AU1162_Lt3Hxb.pdf?source=pqars))\n\nThis means LearnSphere must distinguish genuine product-experience problems from content relevance, motivation, scheduling, trust, technical friction, and competition. The study will fill the knowledge gap between overall satisfaction scores and the specific behavioural moments that cause learners to continue, pause, abandon, return, or switch platforms.\n\n---\n\n### **Research Objectives**\nBased on the approved strategic framework, this study will address the following objectives.\n\n**Strategic Objectives:**\n\n1. **Identify** the strongest behavioural and experience-level drivers of learner engagement and course completion in order to prioritise improvements that can increase sustained progress.\n2. **Diagnose** the causes of inactivity and incomplete courses in order to separate product friction from content, motivation, scheduling, trust, value, and competitive barriers.\n3. **Determine** whether LearnSphere’s learning formats, adaptive tools, feedback systems, social-learning elements, and gamification mechanisms create meaningful learning momentum.\n4. **Compare** the needs of current learners, former or inactive learners, potential users, parents or guardians, teachers, and academic administrators in order to prevent incompatible perspectives from being combined into one score.\n5. **Prioritise** the product improvements most likely to increase engagement, completion, return usage, and recommendation.\n6. **Assess** the evidence and capabilities LearnSphere must strengthen before expanding into certifications or institutional partnerships.\n\n**Tactical Objectives:**\n\n1. **Measure** recent learning frequency, course starts, lesson completion, quiz participation, progress consistency, and the point at which learning stopped.\n2. **Map** the learning journey from discovery and onboarding through course selection, lesson participation, progress tracking, interruption, and resumption.\n3. **Identify** whether content relevance, clarity, difficulty, depth, curriculum fit, instructor quality, or perceived usefulness are linked to disengagement.\n4. **Assess** the perceived usefulness of videos, quizzes, practice activities, learning plans, dashboards, reminders, and other instructional formats.\n5. **Determine** whether AI recommendations, adaptive learning, real-time feedback, reminders, and progress dashboards are noticed, understood, trusted, and acted upon.\n6. **Measure** the contribution of peer interaction, instructor access, discussion, badges, streaks, points, challenges, and recognition to continued learning.\n7. **Identify** the primary and secondary reasons learners stop using LearnSphere, fail to complete courses, or decide not to begin.\n8. **Map** competing platforms, offline alternatives, creator-led content, coaching services, institutional systems, and informal peer-learning communities used by respondents.\n9. **Quantify** the likelihood of continuing, returning, recommending, or trying LearnSphere among relevant respondent groups.\n10. **Prioritise** candidate improvements using MaxDiff if LearnSphere supplies a defined list of at least five feature or experience options.\n11. **Assess** switching costs, win-back potential, and the interventions most likely to bring inactive learners back.\n12. **Determine** the evidence parents, teachers, and administrators require regarding trust, safety, learning outcomes, monitoring, curriculum fit, implementation, and institutional support.\n\n---\n\n### **Type of Study**\nWe will conduct a **Mixed Methodology study combining Usage & Attitude, Customer Experience / Learner Experience, Churn / Exit, and Feature Prioritization research**.\n\nThis combination fits LearnSphere because the business decision is not limited to measuring satisfaction or testing one new feature. LearnSphere needs behavioural evidence, experience diagnosis, lifecycle comparison, stakeholder-specific insight, and a prioritised improvement roadmap. The study will therefore measure what respondents did, where the learning journey breaks down, why engagement stops, and which improvements have the greatest relative value.\n\n---\n\n### **Survey Design Approach**\nThe selected design is a **linear modular questionnaire with role-based routing and a general-to-specific funnel**. The study currently contains **zero discrete stimuli**: content relevance, adaptive learning, feedback, social learning, and gamification are experience domains rather than defined screens, prototypes, concepts, or competing assets. Under the methodology decision framework, cell-based designs are therefore not appropriate at this stage.\n\nThe questionnaire will begin with recent behaviour and usage context, then diagnose the learning journey, followed by satisfaction and engagement drivers, stakeholder-specific modules, and improvement priorities. Current learners will receive the full experience path. Former or inactive learners will be routed toward their last active stage and drop-off reasons. Potential users will answer awareness, expectations, alternatives, and trial-barrier questions rather than rating experiences they have not had. Parents, teachers, and administrators will receive role-appropriate modules.\n\nIf LearnSphere provides five or more clearly defined candidate improvements, a **MaxDiff / Best-Worst Scaling** exercise should be added to create a relative priority hierarchy. MaxDiff is preferred to a long rating grid because it forces trade-offs and reduces the risk that respondents rate every feature as important.\n\n**Alternatives considered:**\n\n- **Sequential Monadic:** Not chosen because there are no two to four discrete concepts, prototypes, or product variants for respondents to evaluate independently.\n- **Pure Monadic or Proto-Monadic:** Not chosen because the current decision is about diagnosing experience and behaviour, not comparing isolated stimuli across cells.\n- **Conjoint / Choice-Based Conjoint:** Not chosen because LearnSphere is not yet asking respondents to trade off defined product configurations, attribute levels, and prices.\n- **Standalone CSAT or NPS:** Not chosen because a single satisfaction or advocacy score would not explain completion barriers, churn triggers, or feature priorities.\n- **Kano Model:** Not selected for the initial design because the feature list is not yet defined; it may be considered later for roadmap classification into must-have, performance, and delight features.\n\n**Survey Routing Logic:**\n\n- **Skip Logic:** Irrelevant questions will be bypassed based on LearnSphere familiarity, usage status, stakeholder role, and recent activity.\n- **Branching:** Current learners, former learners, potential users, parents or guardians, teachers, and administrators will follow separate relevant modules.\n- **Display Logic:** Follow-up questions will appear only when a respondent has the relevant experience or selected the relevant activity.\n- **Termination Logic:** Respondents without sufficient LearnSphere familiarity or relevant learning experience will be screened out gracefully.\n- **Filter Logic:** Follow-ups may focus on platforms, features, or barriers previously selected by the respondent.\n\n**Quality Controls:** Attention checks will be embedded at approximately a 1:9 ratio, with speedster detection below 40-50% of median completion time, straight-line detection across rating batteries, open-ended gibberish screening, and randomisation of non-ordered lists. The survey will use short mobile-friendly screens and avoid long agree/disagree grids.\n\nYou can request a different methodology before generating the survey.\n\n---\n\n### **Question Flow & Survey Phases**\n\n- **Phase 1 — Warm-Up and Screening:** Establish respondent role, LearnSphere familiarity, current or past usage, and recent learning activity. This confirms eligibility and routes respondents to the correct module.\n- **Phase 2 — Learning Context and Goals:** Capture learning objective, course type, expected outcome, study frequency, and the role of parents, teachers, or administrators where relevant. This provides context for interpreting completion and engagement.\n- **Phase 3 — Usage and Completion Behaviour:** Measure recent login or learning frequency, course starts, lesson completion, quiz participation, progress consistency, and the point at which learning stopped. This separates reported attitudes from observed behaviour.\n- **Phase 4 — Learning Journey and Experience Diagnosis:** Assess discovery, onboarding, course selection, navigation, lesson length, technical interruptions, scheduling friction, content relevance, curriculum fit, instructor quality, and resumption of unfinished learning.\n- **Phase 5 — Engagement Drivers and Learning Support:** Examine motivation, progress visibility, feedback, adaptive learning, AI recommendations, reminders, instructor support, social learning, gamification, workload, confidence, and perceived learning progress.\n- **Phase 6 — Role-Specific Modules:** Route potential users to awareness and trial barriers; former learners to churn and win-back questions; parents or guardians to trust, safety, payment, and progress evidence; teachers and administrators to curriculum fit, monitoring, workload, feedback, implementation, and partnership needs.\n- **Phase 7 — Improvement Priorities and Close:** Capture the most important improvement, likelihood to continue or return, likelihood to recommend, open-ended suggestions, and MaxDiff prioritisation if a defined feature list is supplied.\n\nThe expected main learner path is approximately 18-25 questions and 8-12 minutes. Role-specific paths will be shorter where possible. Break screens may be inserted between major phases if the final questionnaire becomes lengthy.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Data Points to Measure**\n\n**Primary Metrics:**\n\n- **Active-learning frequency** - enables segmentation by engagement intensity and comparison of light, moderate, and heavy learners.\n- **Course-start-to-completion behaviour** - identifies where learners progress, pause, or abandon courses.\n- **Completion barriers** - quantifies the relative contribution of content, time, motivation, technology, navigation, value, trust, and competing alternatives.\n- **Content relevance** - tests the hypothesis that learners who see strong relevance to their goals are more likely to continue and complete courses.\n- **Learning-journey satisfaction** - identifies the stages of discovery, onboarding, navigation, lesson use, quizzes, and resumption that require improvement.\n- **Perceived learning progress** - measures whether respondents believe LearnSphere is helping them achieve their intended outcome.\n- **Feedback usefulness and timeliness** - tests whether feedback reduces uncertainty and supports continued progress.\n- **Adaptive-tool awareness, usefulness, and trust** - separates exposure to AI or personalised tools from actual behavioural value.\n- **Social-learning value** - measures the contribution of peers, discussions, instructor access, and community support.\n- **Gamification contribution** - determines whether streaks, points, badges, challenges, and recognition create meaningful momentum or only superficial activity.\n- **Likelihood to continue** - provides a forward-looking retention indicator for current learners.\n- **Likelihood to return** - measures win-back potential among former or inactive learners.\n- **Likelihood to recommend and NPS** - provides an advocacy benchmark alongside diagnostic reason probes.\n- **Primary churn reason** - identifies the single most influential trigger for inactivity or abandonment.\n- **Alternative-platform usage** - maps competitive and offline substitutes that divide learning time.\n- **Feature priority utility** - produces a relative improvement hierarchy through MaxDiff when five or more candidate improvements are supplied.\n\n**Supporting Metrics:**\n\n- Learning objective and expected outcome - provides context for differences in engagement and completion.\n- Course type and subject area - allows comparison of needs across learning missions.\n- Frequency of quizzes and practice - helps determine whether active practice is associated with sustained progress.\n- Lesson-length preference and scheduling constraints - distinguishes motivation barriers from time and workload friction.\n- Course discovery and selection difficulty - identifies acquisition and onboarding issues.\n- Technical interruptions and device or connectivity friction - separates operational barriers from content dissatisfaction.\n- Trust in recommendations and progress dashboards - provides context for adoption of personalised tools.\n- Parent or guardian influence - measures household approval, payment, safety, credibility, and progress-monitoring requirements.\n- Teacher and administrator expectations - assesses curriculum fit, implementation readiness, data visibility, and institutional support needs.\n- Awareness and consideration among potential users - identifies acquisition and positioning gaps.\n- Return or trial requirements - identifies the proof points needed to restart or begin using LearnSphere.\n\n**Advanced Analytics We’ll Apply:**\n\n- **Cross-tabulation and significance testing** to compare current, former, potential, parent or guardian, teacher, and administrator groups.\n- **Driver analysis** to identify which experience factors most strongly relate to continuation, completion, return, and recommendation.\n- **Journey-stage gap analysis** to compare expected and experienced performance across discovery, onboarding, learning, feedback, and resumption.\n- **Churn segmentation** to distinguish content-led, time-led, motivation-led, technology-led, value-led, and competition-led inactivity.\n- **MaxDiff preference shares** to rank candidate feature or experience improvements when a defined item list is available.\n- **Open-ended thematic and sentiment analysis** to identify recurring emotional and functional barriers.\n- **Lifecycle comparison** to identify differences between current learners, former learners, and potential users.\n- **Stakeholder role comparison** to prevent learner, household, educator, and administrator perspectives from being combined into a misleading overall average.\n\n---\n\n### **Expected Insights & Outcomes**\nBy the end of this study, you will possess the data to:\n\n- **Validate** whether perceived content relevance is associated with sustained usage and course completion.\n- **Validate** whether clear progress visibility and timely feedback reduce uncertainty and support continued learning.\n- **Validate** whether adaptive learning tools improve engagement only when learners understand and trust the recommendations.\n- **Validate** whether gamification and social learning influence completion differently across learner types and learning goals.\n- **Validate** whether former learners are more likely to have stopped because of content, time, journey, motivation, value, trust, or competition barriers than because of one isolated feature problem.\n- **Validate** whether parents, teachers, and administrators evaluate LearnSphere using materially different success criteria from learners.\n- **Validate** whether potential users require stronger proof of learning outcomes, credibility, relevance, ease of use, or value before trying LearnSphere.\n- **Identify** the strongest behavioural and experience-level predictors of course engagement and completion.\n- **Quantify** friction across onboarding, course discovery, lessons, quizzes, progress tracking, feedback, adaptive tools, social learning, gamification, and resuming unfinished learning.\n- **Diagnose** why former or inactive learners stopped using LearnSphere and distinguish recoverable users from low-probability win-back segments.\n- **Compare** current learners, former learners, potential users, parents or guardians, teachers, and administrators without blending incompatible perspectives.\n- **Determine** whether AI-powered learning tools, progress tracking, social learning, and gamification are noticed, trusted, valued, and linked to continued use.\n- **Prioritise** the next product-release improvements using MaxDiff if LearnSphere supplies a defined set of candidate features.\n- **Decide** which learner-experience problems should receive priority to improve engagement and course completion.\n- **Decide** whether the next release should focus more heavily on content relevance, learning design, feedback and progress visibility, adaptive learning, social features, gamification, or journey friction.\n- **Decide** which inactive learners are realistically recoverable and what intervention could bring them back.\n- **Decide** what evidence and capabilities LearnSphere must strengthen before expanding into certifications or institutional partnerships.\n\n**KPIs to report:** Active-learning frequency, course-start-to-completion behaviour, completion barriers, learner-experience satisfaction, content relevance, ease of navigation, perceived learning progress, feedback usefulness, adaptive-tool usefulness and trust, social-learning value, gamification contribution, likelihood to continue, likelihood to return, likelihood to recommend, NPS, primary churn reasons, alternative-platform usage, win-back potential, potential-user trial requirements, stakeholder-specific success criteria, and prioritised improvement utilities where MaxDiff is included.\n\n---\n\n### **Methodology** [See why](hercules://explain?topic=Methodology)\nWe will deploy an **approximately 22-question main-path survey taking 8-12 minutes** on the **SuperJ mobile application and the web** to **500** verified Indian respondents. The survey will use a linear modular design with role-based routing rather than stimulus cells.\n\nThe recommended sample is large enough to provide a directional overall read with approximately 5% margin-of-error-style precision at a 95% confidence level under simple random-sample assumptions. The sample is intended for strategic diagnosis and subgroup comparison; stakeholder-role findings should be interpreted directionally unless larger role-specific quotas are commissioned.\n\nThe questionnaire will combine fully labelled 5-point scales, behavioural frequency measures, forced-choice prioritisation where appropriate, targeted open-ended probes, NPS with reason questions, and role-specific routing. If a defined list of five or more candidate improvements is supplied, MaxDiff will be added as a prioritisation module rather than a stimulus-cell test.\n\n**Data Collection Approach:** SuperJ mobile application and web panel, with audience targeting enforced before survey start. Age, gender, city, and NCCS will not be recollected in the questionnaire.\n\n**Quality Controls:** Embedded attention checks, speeder removal, straight-liner detection, randomised non-ordered lists, open-ended gibberish filtering, termination logic for ineligible respondents, and separate reporting by LearnSphere relationship and stakeholder role.\n\n---\n\n### **Target Audience** [See why](hercules://explain?topic=Target+Audience)\nThe target audience will include verified Indian adults who can provide relevant evidence about LearnSphere or comparable digital-learning experiences. The sample will deliberately separate current learners, former or inactive learners, potential users, parents or guardians, teachers or instructors, and academic administrators. This structure reflects the approved framework: learners provide primary experience and completion evidence, while other stakeholders answer role-specific questions rather than being combined into a single learner satisfaction score.\n\n**Total Sample Size:** 500 respondents\n\n**Geographic Focus:** Delhi, Mumbai, Bangalore, and Pune. The four-city spread provides focused coverage across major Indian digital-learning markets without scattering the sample across too many locations.\n\n**Demographics:**\n\n- **Gender:** 50% male / 50% female.\n- **Age Range:** 18-24, 24-35, and 35-45. The proposed distribution is 200 respondents aged 18-24, 180 aged 24-35, and 120 aged 35-45.\n- **Socioeconomic Class:** NCCS A1, A2, B1, and B2, with the proposed split of A1 20%, A2 30%, B1 30%, and B2 20%.\n\n**Behavioural and Stakeholder Filters:**\n\n- 40% current LearnSphere learners with recent platform activity.\n- 20% former or inactive LearnSphere learners who stopped using the platform or did not complete a course.\n- 20% potential users who fit a relevant learning need but do not currently use LearnSphere.\n- 10% parents or guardians who influence a learner’s education decision.\n- 10% teachers, instructors, or academic administrators involved in learning delivery, monitoring, curriculum, or institutional decisions.\n\n**Screening Criteria:** Respondents must be aged 18 or above, reside in India, confirm their relevant stakeholder role, and demonstrate sufficient familiarity with LearnSphere or comparable digital learning to answer the routed module. Current and former learners must report recent or past platform use. Potential users must have a relevant learning need and experience with at least one alternative learning route. Parents, teachers, instructors, and administrators must have direct influence over or professional involvement in learning decisions, delivery, monitoring, or implementation.\n\nThe sample splits are independent dimensions of the same 500 respondents: gender totals 500, age totals 500, and city totals 500. Stakeholder-role percentages are routing quotas and may overlap where a respondent legitimately holds more than one relevant role.","research_framework":null,"context_request":null,"qna_context":null,"audience_payload":{"title":"Keep Learning: The LearnSphere Experience","created_by":"hercules","total":500,"male":250,"female":250,"Nccs":{"A1":"20%","A2":"30%","B1":"30%","B2":"20%"},"Attributes":{"Current LearnSphere learners":"40%","Former or inactive LearnSphere learners":"20%","Potential digital-learning users":"20%","Parents or guardians":"10%","Teachers, instructors, or academic administrators":"10%"},"checkCity":true,"18-24":{"total":200},"24-35":{"total":180},"35-45":{"total":120},"Delhi":{"total":125,"tier":"tier1"},"Mumbai":{"total":125,"tier":"tier1"},"Bangalore":{"total":125,"tier":"tier1"},"Pune":{"total":125,"tier":"tier1"},"previousstate":{}},"cta_buttons":["Yes, create the survey."],"end_message":"Please review the methodology and audience structure before moving to survey generation. You can still change the sample size, city mix, demographic splits, stakeholder quotas, or feature-prioritization module at this stage.","see_why":{"methodology":"**A mixed-method, modular survey** was chosen because LearnSphere needs to connect learner behaviour—such as course starts, pauses, and completion—with the underlying experience drivers, including content relevance, feedback, adaptive tools, social learning, and gamification. **Role-based routing and optional MaxDiff** ensure that each respondent evaluates only relevant experiences while producing actionable priorities for improving engagement, retention, and future certification or partnership readiness.","target_audience":"The audience is deliberately segmented across **current learners, inactive learners, potential users, and influential stakeholders** because each group reveals a different part of LearnSphere’s growth challenge: engagement, churn, acquisition, trust, implementation, or institutional adoption. Focusing on verified Indian respondents in major digital-learning markets, with defined demographic and stakeholder quotas, provides a practical directional view while preventing learner, parent, educator, and administrator perspectives from being blended into misleading overall averages."},"superJSurveyId":"PENDING_b056a10b-f37f-4c3c-b018-c3640d12a976","superJSurveyName":"New Chat (2026-08-14 09:08)","businessId":"6a7edac815203e92a622fda3","error":null,"changes_detected":true,"log_message":null,"exhausted":false,"usage_info":{"tier_type":"FREE","daily_limit":null,"tokens_used_today":0,"tokens_remaining":0,"chat_context_limit":400000,"chat_tokens_used":0,"chat_tokens_remaining":400000,"monthly_chat_limit":10,"chats_created_this_month":1,"chats_remaining":9,"exhausted":false,"exhausted_reason":null},"business_flow":null,"routing_logic":null,"route_logs":[],"urls_referred":[]},"require_auth":false}

